WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.304
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.489 --> 00:00:08.530
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Thank
you for joining us today.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:10.870
Today, my colleague
Wyatt and I are

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:13.360
going to talk about
high-performance computing

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:14.410
on Google Cloud.

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:15.361
My name is Emma.

00:00:15.361 --> 00:00:17.485
I'm a developer advocate
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:34.180
So we have four chapters today.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:39.070
The first part is about
reviewing what HPC is

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:41.230
and what it means on Cloud.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:44.290
And then we'd like to talk about
some of the Cloud offerings

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:48.160
we have for HPC applications.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.840
Then Wyatt be discussing
job management on Cloud

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.620
and some of the
real-world case studies.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:59.830
So let's take a close
look at what HPC is

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:01.540
and what it means on Cloud.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.160
High-performance
computing, or HPC,

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.980
is generally characterized
as massively parallel

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:12.840
and with a lot of data.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.470
So massively parallel
means a lot of cores

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:18.500
from tens of thousands
of cores and sometimes

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.680
hundreds of thousands of cores.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.580
A lot of data usually means
petabytes, sometimes exabytes,

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.890
of data.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:32.900
HPC is one of the most important
areas in computer science.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.300
It powers a lot of
different industry,

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:39.950
such as scientific
simulations, engineering,

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:44.600
financial services, health and
life science, data analysis

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.460
and more.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:48.410
So these are just
a few examples.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.330
But when you think of
high-performance computing,

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:59.680
there are actually two slightly
different types of workloads.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.750
The first kind,
or the first type,

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:04.270
is high throughput
or what we call

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:07.650
embarrassingly
parallel computing.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:13.370
So these types of workloads
are relatively independently

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:19.320
working on each node, so they
communicate less often or less

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.970
often between nodes.

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:27.630
The example, types of workloads
include financial services,

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:32.380
genomics, media transcoding,
rendering, and data analysis.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:36.741
So these types of workloads
just works fine on Cloud

00:02:36.741 --> 00:02:38.365
today, and there are
a lot of examples.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.350
The other type of workload
is tightly coupled

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:47.800
or just parallel computing.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.890
This is more of the
traditional area

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:52.870
in high-performance computing.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:57.190
Applications might use MPI
as a programming interface.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:00.800
They often communicate
between nodes,

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.860
so they require low-latency,
high-bandwidth network

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:07.900
and, of course, they also
require a lot of storage,

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:11.630
and they must be fast enough.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:15.920
So this is more about scientific
engineering computation.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.020
Example use cases
are computation

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:22.490
of fluid dynamics, electronics,
engineering and product

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:25.280
design, oil and gas
research, weather, physics,

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.840
and chemistry.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:33.720
We actually have a lot of
real-use cases and customers

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.930
running these two
types of workloads,

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.080
including the
high-throughput computing

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.870
and tightly-coupled computing.

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:48.590
And while we showcasing some of
the examples during this talk.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:56.770
HPC requires a quite different
kind of infrastructure when

00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:01.330
compared to web applications
or mobile backhands.

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:05.050
Of course, it requires a
strong computation power

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.480
and a lot of storage.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.750
And as they communicate
between nodes,

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.150
they require low-latency
and high-bandwidth network.

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:20.769
And as we will need to
manage all of the nodes,

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:25.930
we need some sort of software
to manage these HPC deployments.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:28.075
And of course, we need
to be cost friendly.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:36.540
Why would we need to use
Cloud or migrate to Cloud?

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:41.160
Well, in the world of
on-premise HPC clusters,

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.190
we usually have
relatively smaller number

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:45.720
of configurations.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.795
For example, we might have
a single configuration

00:04:48.795 --> 00:04:53.970
of nodes with CPUs and GPUs
and the same amount of memory

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:56.700
for all of the nodes.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:02.090
So they usually have one or a
few queues so all of the users

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:07.100
have to use the same
configuration, the same queue.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.710
Sometimes these machines
are too big or too small

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:14.315
for these workloads, so there
are some computation resources

00:05:14.315 --> 00:05:15.190
that are left unused.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:24.830
But with Cloud, you can
configure as many clusters

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:29.440
or as many queues as you need.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:34.865
And on top of that, you can
resize these virtual machines

00:05:34.865 --> 00:05:39.150
as necessary and add SSDs
or GPUs as necessary.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:43.680
For example, if some of
your users need just CPUs,

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:47.010
we can set up a
cluster with CPUs.

00:05:47.010 --> 00:05:52.320
And if another user
might need GPUs,

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.730
we can add GPUs
just for the user.

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:59.490
So these are more resource
efficient and cost efficient.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:02.100
And of course, these clusters
can be scaled in and out

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.196
as necessary.

00:06:04.196 --> 00:06:07.660
And we can go even farther.

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:11.760
For example, when your
task needs exactly 20 CPUs

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:15.820
and 46-gigabytes memory, we
can provision virtual machines

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:18.230
of the exact size.

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:19.960
We call it custom machine types.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:22.300
We'll explain later,
but this is the idea

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:25.630
of having virtual machines and
computation nodes on Cloud.

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.140
So let me introduce some
of the Cloud offerings

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:36.440
we have for HPC applications.

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:37.450
Compute Engine.

00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:41.340
The Compute Engine is one of
the most fundamental components

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.320
on Google Cloud.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:46.560
They're high-performance
virtual machines with access

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.540
to the latest
generations of hardware,

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:53.140
including Intel's Skylake
processors and NVIDIA's Volta

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:55.650
GPUs.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:57.930
They are very easy to provision.

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:03.790
They can be started within
10 seconds or 20 seconds.

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:06.500
So when you are
starting 100 nodes,

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:12.750
they will be ready
within a minute.

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:16.390
And we have the web interface
command line utility

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:21.010
and web API, so you can start
using the Cloud with the web

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:21.830
interface.

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:24.160
And when you need to
automate certain tasks,

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:30.870
you can migrate to other
methods to control your cluster.

00:07:33.830 --> 00:07:37.380
Of course, we take security
very, very seriously.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:42.800
We have more than 750 engineers
and security experts working

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:45.450
to protect our infrastructure.

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:48.650
They're not only
working on the software

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:55.410
we run on our platform, but
also the hardware network

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:57.420
and physical infrastructure.

00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:59.720
So they're designing
and engineering

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:01.790
all aspects of our platform.

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:07.710
And we use the same network.

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:10.710
We have one of the strongest
network for public cloud

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.110
providers.

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:15.090
We the same network
for Google Cloud

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:21.450
that we use for our services
like YouTube, Search, Maps,

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:23.680
and all other applications.

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:26.040
So running your
application on Google Cloud

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:32.789
means you will be benefited from
the network and infrastructure

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:34.889
that we use to run our services.

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:42.010
These are some of the
numbers and details.

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:46.890
So Compute Engine
is very flexible--

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:53.640
from 1 core to 160 cores, and
you can scale up to nearly 4

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.070
terabytes of memory.

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:59.970
And you can, of course, attach
GPUs and SSDs as necessary.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:04.510
And you can configure
preemptible VMs and GPUs.

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:06.360
These are mechanisms
to save costs.

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:12.330
I'll explain this part
more in detail later.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.470
Since this is very
flexible, you can

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:18.420
install any operating systems
or any software that you

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:20.040
need to run your software.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:23.910
So Compute Engine can be
used for MPI applications,

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:27.040
batch-HTC applications,
real-time processing,

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:30.170
MapReduce, and machine
learning workloads.

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:33.345
So this is a very flexible.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:40.050
Having access to latest
generation of hardware

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:43.220
is even more
important with Cloud.

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:47.100
With Intel Skylake
CPUs, your application

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:52.560
might run faster by
up to 82% with access

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:57.712
to the AVX-512 instructions.

00:09:57.712 --> 00:10:02.140
Shorter runtime means not only
you get your results faster

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:06.760
but also, you can terminate
these instances earlier,

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:10.460
so you don't have to
pay for the idling time

00:10:10.460 --> 00:10:12.760
and wasted the
computing resources.

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:19.200
We have GPUs as well.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:23.460
You can attach up to eight
GPUs per virtual machine.

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:26.580
They're directly attached
to these virtual machines

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:28.830
via PCI express,
so they are close

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:30.840
to the bare-metal performance.

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:38.090
And GPUs also [INAUDIBLE]
preemptible's virtual machines.

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:48.430
We offer NVIDIA Tesla K80,
P100, and the latest Volta V100.

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:51.910
We have a couple of
blocks switch options.

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:55.110
The first option
is the local SSD.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:58.080
They are directly attached
to the virtual machines.

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:00.960
There are ephemeral, so
when virtual machines

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:05.790
are terminating, the data
on this storage will lost.

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:11.640
But it is very fast with
680 kilo IOPS for read

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:17.550
and 2.5 gigabits per
second of throughput.

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:23.160
You can attach up to 3 terabytes
of SSDs to a single instance.

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:27.080
This is best to be
used for temporary data

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:31.810
and other frequently
access scratch space.

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:40.640
We also have persistent
disks with SSDs and HTDs.

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:43.800
So as the name implies,
these are persistent.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:47.940
So even when virtual
machines are terminated,

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:49.360
the data will be kept.

00:11:54.530 --> 00:11:57.480
SSDs are best for latency
sensitive applications

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:59.540
and data.

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:07.230
They have up to 40k read
IOPS and 100 megabytes power

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:10.300
read of throughput.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:14.320
Hard disks are very
cost effective.

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:18.530
It is best suited for latency
insensitive tasks and lots

00:12:18.530 --> 00:12:21.220
of data.

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:23.530
Both of these
persistent disks can

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:27.670
be scaled up to use 64
terabytes per instance.

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:31.870
And we offer other features
such as Snapshots, encryptions,

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:33.500
and others to protect your data.

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:43.100
We also, for objects storage--

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:49.370
Cloud Storage-- for
large unstructured data.

00:12:49.370 --> 00:12:52.760
These can scale up
to exabytes and can

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:55.280
be accessed from any of
the virtual machines,

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:58.520
and even from outside of
a Google Cloud Platform

00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:00.890
with the REST interface.

00:13:00.890 --> 00:13:03.340
The latency with
the objects storage

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:06.770
is higher than persistent
disks, so they're more

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:11.180
suited for bulk operations.

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:16.070
We offer POSIX-compliant
storage with gscfuse,

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:21.460
so you can mount
these storage buckets

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:23.220
from your Linux instances.

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:32.500
We have some of
the best networks.

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:35.530
You can launch up to
7,000 virtual machines

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:39.550
in a single network.

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:43.390
And they have relatively
predictable low latency

00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:47.920
between nodes, with
40 to 60 microseconds.

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:53.080
The bandwidth depends
on the number of cores.

00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:56.150
They can scale up to
16 gigabits per second.

00:13:56.150 --> 00:14:00.930
So this is faster than
10-gigabit ethernet.

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:03.090
And in order to achieve
this performance,

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:08.070
we design our data center
network from scratch.

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:12.480
We use closed approach network
so that these nodes can

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:17.370
communicate to other nodes
relatively regardless

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:19.460
of their physical positions.

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:26.580
Not only the intra-data
center network,

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:31.200
we also have a global
Google network.

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:36.750
So you will seeing this
diagram before I think.

00:14:36.750 --> 00:14:39.360
But these blue lines
are existing cables

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:43.920
that Google uses,
and these green lines

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:49.810
are new optic fiber cables
that we invest in and build

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:51.250
with our partners.

00:14:51.250 --> 00:14:55.620
So when you are working
with other institutions

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:57.630
and working on
international projects,

00:14:57.630 --> 00:15:00.800
you can move your data from
one continent to another.

00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:09.430
We offer options to save cost.

00:15:09.430 --> 00:15:10.640
Custom machine types--

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:14.410
I talked about this
briefly before.

00:15:14.410 --> 00:15:19.510
This is a mechanism to
pick any configuration that

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:22.690
matches your workloads.

00:15:22.690 --> 00:15:27.090
This is as easy as moving these
slides in the web interface.

00:15:27.090 --> 00:15:31.560
You can choose from
1 core to 106 cores

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:37.650
very easily by moving the slides
and the same for the memory.

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:43.560
Our customers have saved an
average of 90% or up to 50%

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:46.220
by using custom machine types.

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:49.860
So this is about choosing the
right size of virtual machines

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:51.060
for your workloads.

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:55.935
These numbers are from the
real customer examples.

00:15:58.610 --> 00:16:02.600
And we also, for preemptive
virtual machine instances,

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:07.490
they are discounted
by up to 80%,

00:16:07.490 --> 00:16:12.760
so they are super low
cost, short-term instances.

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:15.570
So they might get preempted.

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:20.460
So they have the maximum
lifetime of 24 hours.

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:25.940
They might run up to 24 hours
but they are not guaranteed

00:16:25.940 --> 00:16:30.080
to be alive for 24 hours.

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:34.640
We might have to
terminate them, but they

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:39.510
are discounted in return.

00:16:39.510 --> 00:16:45.210
And we have reduced pricing
for attached resources,

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:48.220
such as local SSDs and GPUs.

00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:54.690
So if you run preemptible
VMs, your attached resources

00:16:54.690 --> 00:16:57.880
are also discounted.

00:16:57.880 --> 00:16:59.970
And we even, with
preemptible VMs,

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:02.280
you have access to the
same set of hardware

00:17:02.280 --> 00:17:06.380
that we offer for regular VMs.

00:17:06.380 --> 00:17:10.460
So this is best suited for
relatively shorter lifetime

00:17:10.460 --> 00:17:16.640
tasks and high-throughput
computing or independent jobs

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:17.780
kind of tasks.

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:22.790
They can either be
started again from scratch

00:17:22.790 --> 00:17:28.010
or can be resumed
from checkpoints.

00:17:28.010 --> 00:17:31.400
So even if you have to
run the same task again,

00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:32.780
they are discounted farther.

00:17:32.780 --> 00:17:34.990
So in total, you can save cost.

00:17:38.230 --> 00:17:41.560
Now, I'd like to welcome Wyatt
to talk about job management

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:43.550
and real-use cases on Cloud.

00:17:46.707 --> 00:17:48.000
WYATT GORMAN: Hi, everybody.

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:49.380
Thank you, Emma.

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:51.330
So Emma's just given
us a great overview

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:53.280
of the infrastructure
that we could

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:55.980
use to build high-performance
computing clouds.

00:17:55.980 --> 00:18:00.477
So I want to talk now about
how we can run and manage jobs

00:18:00.477 --> 00:18:02.310
on those high-performance
computing clusters

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:04.240
in the Cloud, what
software we can use--

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:06.240
I want to give you all a
couple of opportunities

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:07.590
to get your hands
dirty, so if you

00:18:07.590 --> 00:18:09.250
want to take your laptops
out, I have a couple of links

00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:11.625
so you can load up some
codelabs and tutorials--

00:18:11.625 --> 00:18:13.500
and then I want to talk
about some real world

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:16.320
customer-use cases where we've
seen our customers running

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:18.778
their high-performance computing
applications in the cloud.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:24.090
So we have a number of
workload management partners.

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:25.920
So first of all, what
is a workload manager

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:27.720
in high-performance computing?

00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:30.450
In traditional high-performance
computing, a workload manager,

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:34.350
their job is pretty much
limited to managing resources

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:36.810
and allocating jobs
throughout a cluster.

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:38.560
So that means that you
build your cluster,

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:40.410
you configure it with your
operating system, everything

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:42.493
like that, and then you
install a workload manager

00:18:42.493 --> 00:18:43.260
on top of that.

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:46.230
And then you use that workload
manager to submit jobs,

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:48.600
and that workload manager
allocates those jobs

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:52.320
throughout the cluster to manage
those resources and best bin

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:54.090
pack it's called, right?

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:57.330
So in the Cloud, the role
of a workload manager

00:18:57.330 --> 00:18:59.220
expands a little bit often.

00:18:59.220 --> 00:19:02.040
And so they often take
on the responsibility

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:04.350
of building the cluster,
provisioning the nodes,

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:07.050
configuring the nodes even,
and everything like that.

00:19:07.050 --> 00:19:08.883
Those are the sorts of
things that you can't

00:19:08.883 --> 00:19:11.010
do with hardware and software.

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:15.450
So they even extend often to
scheduling maintenance tasks

00:19:15.450 --> 00:19:17.910
and tearing down
nodes as needed.

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:19.890
So we have a number of
partners, as I said.

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:22.900
I want to just focus
on three of them today.

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:26.610
So the three we have today
are Altair's PBScloud.io.

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:31.200
This is a really easy-to-use,
web-based management system.

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:33.960
We have SchedMD Slurm,
and we also have HTCondor.

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:35.970
So for the people
who are here, who's

00:19:35.970 --> 00:19:40.290
used these pieces of software
somewhere on premise already?

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:41.040
Just a few people.

00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:41.370
Great.

00:19:41.370 --> 00:19:43.950
This is an intro course, so
you're all in the right place.

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:45.597
Who's used it on
the Cloud, though--

00:19:45.597 --> 00:19:46.930
any of these pieces of software?

00:19:46.930 --> 00:19:49.289
A couple of hands went up
that didn't go up earlier.

00:19:49.289 --> 00:19:49.830
That's great.

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:50.991
Warms my heart.

00:19:50.991 --> 00:19:53.490
So I'm hoping to get everybody's
hands up by the end of this

00:19:53.490 --> 00:19:56.070
because we have a couple
of tutorials and codelabs

00:19:56.070 --> 00:19:58.110
we can do.

00:19:58.110 --> 00:19:59.670
So first of all is
Altair's PBScloud.

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:01.760
For anybody who isn't
familiar with Altair,

00:20:01.760 --> 00:20:03.990
they're a company
that develops software

00:20:03.990 --> 00:20:06.840
for things like
computational fluid dynamics,

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:09.600
structural simulations
for engineering,

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:13.920
crash simulations in
automotive and military uses.

00:20:13.920 --> 00:20:16.830
So they have this
suite of software,

00:20:16.830 --> 00:20:18.960
and they really wanted
to build a system that

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:22.590
would allow their customers
to run these jobs very easily

00:20:22.590 --> 00:20:25.080
using the Cloud resources.

00:20:25.080 --> 00:20:29.220
So what they built
was PBScloud.io,

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:31.510
and they built it on GCP.

00:20:31.510 --> 00:20:35.190
So PBScloud.io, like I said,
is a very simple-to-use,

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:40.180
web-based GUI where you can log
in, when you log in you provide

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:42.092
a Google Cloud
Platform billing ID--

00:20:42.092 --> 00:20:44.550
so that's the only tie-back
you really have to Google Cloud

00:20:44.550 --> 00:20:47.050
to know that you're
running on Google Cloud.

00:20:47.050 --> 00:20:48.720
So you provide this
billing ID, and then

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:52.530
you get a menu of possible
solvers, Altair software,

00:20:52.530 --> 00:20:55.680
and others, and you can
provide your input data,

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:57.960
you can provide the way
you want the job to scale,

00:20:57.960 --> 00:21:00.330
any limits or anything like
that you want to apply,

00:21:00.330 --> 00:21:01.530
and click launch.

00:21:01.530 --> 00:21:03.570
And it will go and
spin up the nodes,

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:05.610
it'll show you that
it's spinning them up,

00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:07.950
configuring them, and
then actually allocating

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:09.270
your job across those nodes.

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:10.728
Tear the whole
thing down once it's

00:21:10.728 --> 00:21:13.380
done, provide your output data
into a Google Cloud Storage

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:14.940
bucket, for example.

00:21:14.940 --> 00:21:16.440
So this is a really,
really easy way

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:18.420
to get started
with some workloads

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:22.200
that you already have
that are independent.

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:24.420
So you can go and
log on to PBScloud.io

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:28.260
today, provide your billing
ID, and start launching jobs.

00:21:28.260 --> 00:21:31.050
So that's a very simple
way to get going.

00:21:31.050 --> 00:21:33.840
That's not typical
in HPC though.

00:21:33.840 --> 00:21:37.660
So Slurm is another more
traditional HPC workload

00:21:37.660 --> 00:21:38.160
manager.

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:39.120
It's very popular.

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:40.140
It's open source.

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:42.598
It's used in some of the largest
high-performance computing

00:21:42.598 --> 00:21:43.956
clusters in the world.

00:21:43.956 --> 00:21:45.330
And so we partnered
with SchedMD,

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:47.550
who are the developers
and maintainers of Slurm,

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:51.720
to bring Google Cloud native
functionality and integrations

00:21:51.720 --> 00:21:54.390
into the Slurm Workload Manager.

00:21:54.390 --> 00:21:57.780
And so we announced this
at SuperComputing 2017.

00:21:57.780 --> 00:22:00.060
These integrations are
available on Slurm's GitHub,

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:01.601
so if you wanted to
Google them right

00:22:01.601 --> 00:22:04.140
now you could go and
look at all the code.

00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:07.020
And so there are three ways
to use these integrations.

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:09.990
The first is a standalone
auto-scaling cluster

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:11.146
in the Cloud.

00:22:11.146 --> 00:22:13.020
The second is a burst
to cloud use case where

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:18.130
you have an existing controller
as an existing Slurm cluster,

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:21.210
and you can actually spin up
independent VMs in a cloud

00:22:21.210 --> 00:22:24.150
project and run jobs
on them as needed.

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:26.154
And the third is a
federate-to-cloud use case

00:22:26.154 --> 00:22:28.320
that's really popular, and
I'll talk about the Cloud

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:31.210
auto-scaling and federate
to cloud a little bit more.

00:22:31.210 --> 00:22:32.980
But for anybody who
wants to get started,

00:22:32.980 --> 00:22:34.050
this is the link right there.

00:22:34.050 --> 00:22:34.925
You can load that up.

00:22:34.925 --> 00:22:36.770
That's a codelab--
a Google codelab--

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:39.300
that will guide you through
setting up a project,

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:41.790
downloading the
Slurm integrations,

00:22:41.790 --> 00:22:44.310
and configuring them
and launching a cluster,

00:22:44.310 --> 00:22:46.950
and then even scaling
it to meet demand

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:49.949
or you could search
"Google Slurm Codelab."

00:22:49.949 --> 00:22:50.740
Everybody got that?

00:22:50.740 --> 00:22:51.490
Got your pictures?

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:52.747
Great.

00:22:52.747 --> 00:22:54.330
So its just down
here at the bottom if

00:22:54.330 --> 00:22:55.870
anybody needs as well.

00:22:55.870 --> 00:22:58.870
So in a Cloud auto-scaling
implementation,

00:22:58.870 --> 00:23:01.780
its like I said-- a standalone
cluster in the cloud.

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:02.750
So what does that mean?

00:23:02.750 --> 00:23:05.880
It means you have a login node,
as is traditional in any HPC

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:08.670
cluster, you want an independent
login node that isn't actually

00:23:08.670 --> 00:23:10.820
doing any work so
that if it goes down

00:23:10.820 --> 00:23:12.570
when you're SSHed into
it for some reason,

00:23:12.570 --> 00:23:13.905
nothing's affected.

00:23:13.905 --> 00:23:15.780
So you have a login
node, a controller node--

00:23:15.780 --> 00:23:17.821
that's the node that's
actually running the Slurm

00:23:17.821 --> 00:23:20.710
controller, the Slurm
database, the Slurm accounting

00:23:20.710 --> 00:23:23.310
daemon, and everything like that
that everything reports back

00:23:23.310 --> 00:23:25.000
into to get its job allocation.

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:26.710
And then you have
the compute nodes.

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:30.160
So all of these are running
on Google Compute Engine.

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:34.225
And so the cluster auto-scales
according to two parameters--

00:23:34.225 --> 00:23:36.070
it scales up and down.

00:23:36.070 --> 00:23:38.570
So the parameters are,
first, job requirements.

00:23:38.570 --> 00:23:41.500
So if any one job is submitted
that needs more resources

00:23:41.500 --> 00:23:44.160
than the cluster
currently has, it

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:46.730
will spin up resources
to accommodate that.

00:23:46.730 --> 00:23:49.090
So for example, if you had
a cluster of two nodes--

00:23:49.090 --> 00:23:51.020
two compute nodes, for example--

00:23:51.020 --> 00:23:53.922
and you submitted a single
job that required four nodes,

00:23:53.922 --> 00:23:55.630
it'll spin up those
two additional nodes,

00:23:55.630 --> 00:23:58.000
integrate them into the
cluster, and allocate the job

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:00.020
to those four nodes.

00:24:00.020 --> 00:24:01.150
The second is queue depth.

00:24:01.150 --> 00:24:02.590
So this isn't something
that a lot of people

00:24:02.590 --> 00:24:03.620
think about very often.

00:24:03.620 --> 00:24:08.020
But if you were to submit,
say, four single-node jobs,

00:24:08.020 --> 00:24:10.660
each job only requiring
at most one node,

00:24:10.660 --> 00:24:13.420
then a workload manager
might just allocate them

00:24:13.420 --> 00:24:14.467
all to the same node.

00:24:14.467 --> 00:24:16.300
Depending on your
over-subscription settings

00:24:16.300 --> 00:24:20.410
in Slurm though, you can scale
to meet that so those four

00:24:20.410 --> 00:24:22.660
jobs would spin up into
additional two nodes

00:24:22.660 --> 00:24:25.510
to meet the four nodes that were
required by each of those four

00:24:25.510 --> 00:24:27.230
jobs.

00:24:27.230 --> 00:24:30.190
It's a very dynamic scaling
to meet requirements.

00:24:30.190 --> 00:24:32.810
And then like I said, it spins
them down as well afterwards.

00:24:32.810 --> 00:24:35.710
So according to an
adjustable idle timeout,

00:24:35.710 --> 00:24:39.130
the nodes will be deprovisioned
and deleted in Google Cloud

00:24:39.130 --> 00:24:41.170
after the nodes are
idle so that you're not

00:24:41.170 --> 00:24:44.380
paying for any unneeded,
unnecessary usage

00:24:44.380 --> 00:24:46.150
or compute time.

00:24:46.150 --> 00:24:48.730
The best part of all this is
that once you deploy the Slurm

00:24:48.730 --> 00:24:50.355
cluster in the Cloud,
for anybody who's

00:24:50.355 --> 00:24:52.360
used Slurm before,
it's the same tools

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:55.300
that you used before, it's
the same tools that your users

00:24:55.300 --> 00:25:00.010
and admins are used to, so it's
really easy to get started.

00:25:00.010 --> 00:25:02.530
The second use case is the
federate-to-cloud use case.

00:25:02.530 --> 00:25:04.930
This is where we see a
lot of customers going

00:25:04.930 --> 00:25:07.580
for their burst-to-cloud needs.

00:25:07.580 --> 00:25:12.040
So in a Slurm federation, you're
able to add multiple clusters

00:25:12.040 --> 00:25:14.200
into what's called a
federation and distribute jobs

00:25:14.200 --> 00:25:15.820
amongst them.

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:19.540
So you combine all
of these clusters,

00:25:19.540 --> 00:25:23.530
you actually use a single
accounting master server

00:25:23.530 --> 00:25:25.330
for all of the Slurm
accounting, which

00:25:25.330 --> 00:25:29.350
makes things like Slurm
account management

00:25:29.350 --> 00:25:31.600
as well as job placement
policies and things

00:25:31.600 --> 00:25:33.670
like that, which are
very powerful in Slurm.

00:25:33.670 --> 00:25:36.370
Makes it really easy to manage
all those from a single master

00:25:36.370 --> 00:25:37.540
server.

00:25:37.540 --> 00:25:40.330
And then you can actually
manually assign jobs

00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:41.740
to independent clusters.

00:25:41.740 --> 00:25:44.110
If, for example, you knew
one user was supposed

00:25:44.110 --> 00:25:46.060
to run on just one
cluster, you can

00:25:46.060 --> 00:25:48.560
mandate that they only
run on that cluster

00:25:48.560 --> 00:25:51.910
or you can allow the federation
to allocate that job.

00:25:51.910 --> 00:25:54.820
And how that works is that the
federation actually submits

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:56.590
the job to all the clusters.

00:25:56.590 --> 00:25:58.780
So all the clusters
get a view of this job,

00:25:58.780 --> 00:26:03.160
and whichever cluster responds
that it can allocate the job

00:26:03.160 --> 00:26:06.820
the fastest is given that job,
and all the other clusters

00:26:06.820 --> 00:26:09.790
delete that job replica.

00:26:09.790 --> 00:26:12.310
So a great way to distribute
jobs automatically

00:26:12.310 --> 00:26:16.709
according to whatever is
able to respond quickest.

00:26:16.709 --> 00:26:18.250
So this is really
great, like I said,

00:26:18.250 --> 00:26:19.780
for burst-to-cloud use cases.

00:26:19.780 --> 00:26:23.260
A lot of customers have
already started doing that.

00:26:23.260 --> 00:26:25.390
And it's really
easy to get started.

00:26:25.390 --> 00:26:27.920
It's literally one line in
your Slurm configuration,

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:30.400
you restart the daemons,
and you're up and running.

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:32.800
So it's really easy
to get started.

00:26:32.800 --> 00:26:34.630
There will be
codelab on Federation

00:26:34.630 --> 00:26:36.700
that's linked from the
auto-scaling codelab

00:26:36.700 --> 00:26:39.770
as well very shortly.

00:26:39.770 --> 00:26:42.430
Another example is HTCondor.

00:26:42.430 --> 00:26:44.521
So this is a really
popular workload manager

00:26:44.521 --> 00:26:47.020
that's specifically designed
for high throughput computing--

00:26:47.020 --> 00:26:48.561
the kind of high
throughput computing

00:26:48.561 --> 00:26:50.690
that Emma introduced earlier.

00:26:50.690 --> 00:26:53.170
And so it's really
easily deployed

00:26:53.170 --> 00:26:55.900
on Google Cloud Platform
using our Deployment Manager.

00:26:55.900 --> 00:26:57.610
So we have a tutorial
there at that link.

00:26:57.610 --> 00:27:01.507
You can also just Google search
"Google HTCondor Tutorial,"

00:27:01.507 --> 00:27:03.340
and it'll walk you
through the whole process

00:27:03.340 --> 00:27:05.370
of downloading the
deployment manager scripts,

00:27:05.370 --> 00:27:08.474
installing them, and
launching an HTCondor cluster,

00:27:08.474 --> 00:27:09.890
as well as running
a job on there.

00:27:13.430 --> 00:27:15.920
So now I'd like to go
into some case studies.

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:19.850
So to show you the power
of HTCondor in the Cloud,

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:21.710
we actually have a
great case study.

00:27:21.710 --> 00:27:24.770
So Fermilab is a
national laboratory

00:27:24.770 --> 00:27:27.071
that does research into
high-energy particle physics.

00:27:27.071 --> 00:27:28.820
For anybody who is not
familiar with them,

00:27:28.820 --> 00:27:31.380
they're based near Chicago.

00:27:31.380 --> 00:27:33.590
And so they do a lot
of work with groups

00:27:33.590 --> 00:27:37.550
like CERN and other particle
physics and particle

00:27:37.550 --> 00:27:39.810
accelerator groups.

00:27:39.810 --> 00:27:44.390
And so what they did was,
at SuperComputing 2016,

00:27:44.390 --> 00:27:46.370
during the week of the show--

00:27:46.370 --> 00:27:48.390
on the show floor in fact--

00:27:48.390 --> 00:27:54.810
they burst their resources
by double using Google Cloud

00:27:54.810 --> 00:27:55.700
Platform.

00:27:55.700 --> 00:27:58.770
So in order to accommodate
the, as you can imagine,

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:00.710
massive amounts of
computing that they do,

00:28:00.710 --> 00:28:03.710
they built something called
HEPCloud, H-E-P Cloud--

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:06.200
High Energy Physics Cloud.

00:28:06.200 --> 00:28:09.050
And so this cloud
is really a portal

00:28:09.050 --> 00:28:12.950
designed to coalesce a lot of
disparate computing resources.

00:28:12.950 --> 00:28:16.346
So they borrow computing time
from [INAUDIBLE],, and NCSA,

00:28:16.346 --> 00:28:18.600
and a lot of large
supercomputing centers

00:28:18.600 --> 00:28:21.230
and put it all together
in this HEPCloud

00:28:21.230 --> 00:28:25.010
to run their jobs
since they're so large.

00:28:25.010 --> 00:28:27.410
And they decided to
use GCP to burst.

00:28:27.410 --> 00:28:30.770
So they actually burst in a very
traditional bursty workload--

00:28:30.770 --> 00:28:33.800
a nine-to-five pattern, where
they see their researchers

00:28:33.800 --> 00:28:36.244
coming in and doing their work.

00:28:36.244 --> 00:28:37.910
And they doubled their
compute resources

00:28:37.910 --> 00:28:42.740
from 160,000 cores to 320,000
cores during that nine-to-five

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:43.490
all week.

00:28:43.490 --> 00:28:45.740
And they were able to do
this really cost effectively,

00:28:45.740 --> 00:28:49.250
for only about $1,400 an hour,
because of the preemptible VMs

00:28:49.250 --> 00:28:53.090
and custom machine types that we
have available on Google Cloud.

00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:56.111
So if you'd like to learn a
little bit more about this

00:28:56.111 --> 00:28:58.610
and see architecture diagrams
and everything like that where

00:28:58.610 --> 00:29:00.350
they describe how they
did this, then there's

00:29:00.350 --> 00:29:01.766
a great talk from
last year's Next

00:29:01.766 --> 00:29:04.250
called "Large scale and batch
computing Google Compute

00:29:04.250 --> 00:29:04.957
Engine.

00:29:04.957 --> 00:29:06.040
That's really interesting.

00:29:06.040 --> 00:29:09.620
They have a Fermilab
engineer there to present.

00:29:09.620 --> 00:29:13.070
Just to give you an idea,
this is the project they did.

00:29:13.070 --> 00:29:15.440
It's called the CMS detector.

00:29:15.440 --> 00:29:24.830
It's basically a 14,000-ton, 1
million pixel digital camera,

00:29:24.830 --> 00:29:30.554
and it captures about 40
million images a second.

00:29:30.554 --> 00:29:32.720
So you can imagine the
amount of data that generates

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:36.560
and the amount of
processing that takes.

00:29:36.560 --> 00:29:39.140
Another great example is our
friends, Andrew Sutherland,

00:29:39.140 --> 00:29:40.160
at MIT.

00:29:40.160 --> 00:29:43.080
He's a mathematics
professor and researcher.

00:29:43.080 --> 00:29:45.380
And so he was exploring
the generalization

00:29:45.380 --> 00:29:47.830
of hyperelliptic curves.

00:29:47.830 --> 00:29:49.490
It's a math problem.

00:29:49.490 --> 00:29:54.170
And so he needed a really large
amount of compute resources

00:29:54.170 --> 00:29:55.830
to do this work.

00:29:55.830 --> 00:29:59.480
And so MIT being MIT, they
have some HPC resources,

00:29:59.480 --> 00:30:01.760
so he went to them
and he said, I

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:05.690
need a lot of nodes, a lot
of cores, to do this job,

00:30:05.690 --> 00:30:07.430
and I need some very
specific software.

00:30:07.430 --> 00:30:09.138
And they weren't able
to accommodate him,

00:30:09.138 --> 00:30:11.320
so he needed to go
to Google Cloud.

00:30:11.320 --> 00:30:13.820
And at first, as you can see
in the blog post on the bottom,

00:30:13.820 --> 00:30:17.109
he spun up 220,000
cores for a single job.

00:30:17.109 --> 00:30:19.150
He likes to break his own
records though I guess,

00:30:19.150 --> 00:30:23.970
so the next job he ran, he hit
580,000 cores in a single job

00:30:23.970 --> 00:30:25.820
using preemptible VMs.

00:30:25.820 --> 00:30:28.920
And he did this on
32-core instances--

00:30:28.920 --> 00:30:30.560
18,000 of them or so.

00:30:30.560 --> 00:30:34.770
That came to about 300
core years of compute.

00:30:34.770 --> 00:30:36.410
And he did this
using preemptible VM,

00:30:36.410 --> 00:30:39.140
so he set up checkpointing
on the local disk

00:30:39.140 --> 00:30:42.080
so that when the node was
preempted-- when a node is

00:30:42.080 --> 00:30:43.490
preempted, it's just stopped.

00:30:43.490 --> 00:30:46.250
So you can restart the node, and
if it's check-pointed to disk,

00:30:46.250 --> 00:30:48.770
you resume from the
checkpoint, and off you go.

00:30:48.770 --> 00:30:52.040
So very efficient way
to do that computation.

00:30:52.040 --> 00:30:54.530
He said, "it changes your
whole outlook on research

00:30:54.530 --> 00:30:57.050
when you can ask a question
and get an answer in hours

00:30:57.050 --> 00:30:58.790
rather than months or weeks."

00:30:58.790 --> 00:31:00.950
"You start to really ask
different questions,"

00:31:00.950 --> 00:31:01.940
and that's really true.

00:31:01.940 --> 00:31:04.760
The reason he did that
580,000-core job is because he

00:31:04.760 --> 00:31:07.970
decided to expand his
problem space tremendously,

00:31:07.970 --> 00:31:10.040
to not look at just
the traditional curves,

00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:12.420
but to look at any curves.

00:31:12.420 --> 00:31:14.420
And he got some interesting
results out of that.

00:31:14.420 --> 00:31:17.045
So you can read more about that
in the blog post at the bottom.

00:31:19.020 --> 00:31:23.240
So what does 300 core
years of compute time

00:31:23.240 --> 00:31:24.320
look like in a few hours?

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:26.400
Well, there you have it.

00:31:26.400 --> 00:31:29.330
And the best part of this is
that he actually launched this

00:31:29.330 --> 00:31:32.090
on a Sunday afternoon
sitting on his back porch,

00:31:32.090 --> 00:31:35.000
and it cost less than the amount
of money he expected it to cost

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:35.960
and got in his grant.

00:31:40.130 --> 00:31:42.230
Schlumberger is
another great example.

00:31:42.230 --> 00:31:44.521
Schlumberger, for anybody
who's not familiar with them,

00:31:44.521 --> 00:31:46.280
is an oil and gas
exploration company.

00:31:46.280 --> 00:31:49.910
And so they migrated
their compute resources

00:31:49.910 --> 00:31:52.490
to Google Cloud to meet
their massive demand.

00:31:52.490 --> 00:31:55.100
So what Schlumberger does
is really sends out ships

00:31:55.100 --> 00:31:59.870
into the ocean with massive
trawler signal arrays and data

00:31:59.870 --> 00:32:02.420
capture arrays, and they
use all of that data

00:32:02.420 --> 00:32:05.120
to create visualizations
of the sea's subsurface

00:32:05.120 --> 00:32:07.700
to find these oil deposits
and tell other people where

00:32:07.700 --> 00:32:09.320
to drill.

00:32:09.320 --> 00:32:13.610
And so by using their custom
machine types and NVIDIA GPUs,

00:32:13.610 --> 00:32:17.270
they were able to hit about
35 petaflops of compute power

00:32:17.270 --> 00:32:21.180
and 10 petabytes of storage
in Google Cloud Platform.

00:32:21.180 --> 00:32:23.480
There's a great talk
as well from Next

00:32:23.480 --> 00:32:25.820
'17 called "Supercharge
your performance using

00:32:25.820 --> 00:32:27.380
GPUs in the cloud."

00:32:27.380 --> 00:32:30.290
And during that, Ashok Belani,
the executive vice president

00:32:30.290 --> 00:32:31.732
of technology at
Schlumberger said

00:32:31.732 --> 00:32:33.440
they're "leveraging
the strengths offered

00:32:33.440 --> 00:32:35.660
by cloud computation
to bring their data

00:32:35.660 --> 00:32:37.040
processing to the next level."

00:32:40.670 --> 00:32:43.060
And finally, we have
Silicon Therapeutics.

00:32:43.060 --> 00:32:44.900
They're a drug
discovery company based

00:32:44.900 --> 00:32:46.710
in Cambridge, Massachusetts.

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:52.120
And so they shifted their
compute to run on GCP.

00:32:52.120 --> 00:32:55.010
And they, by doing
so, were able to find

00:32:55.010 --> 00:32:58.410
many more active compounds than
they were able to previously.

00:32:58.410 --> 00:33:01.144
So in the blog post at the
bottom of the slide there

00:33:01.144 --> 00:33:02.810
they said, "the biggest
benefit of using

00:33:02.810 --> 00:33:04.940
GCP was being able
to more thoroughly

00:33:04.940 --> 00:33:08.660
screen compounds than they
ever could have before"

00:33:08.660 --> 00:33:12.320
with their in-house resources,
and that the massive amount

00:33:12.320 --> 00:33:14.510
of compute resources
available in the Cloud

00:33:14.510 --> 00:33:17.600
allowed them to find more
active compounds than they ever

00:33:17.600 --> 00:33:19.440
would have been able
to find previously,

00:33:19.440 --> 00:33:20.856
which means that
they can probably

00:33:20.856 --> 00:33:23.570
find more active drugs.

00:33:23.570 --> 00:33:26.390
So they hit about 16,000
cores in 3 million core hours

00:33:26.390 --> 00:33:28.610
for only $30,000.

00:33:28.610 --> 00:33:32.287
Again, this is because of
preemptible VMs and custom

00:33:32.287 --> 00:33:32.870
machine types.

00:33:32.870 --> 00:33:35.270
They actually used a mix of
standard and preemptible VMs,

00:33:35.270 --> 00:33:36.690
which is very popular.

00:33:36.690 --> 00:33:39.380
So the standard VMs were
used for the longer running

00:33:39.380 --> 00:33:42.210
and static portions
of their jobs.

00:33:42.210 --> 00:33:44.480
Some of those were
coordinating jobs

00:33:44.480 --> 00:33:48.170
where they would launch
sub-jobs or minor jobs

00:33:48.170 --> 00:33:49.700
and then coalesce
all of the results

00:33:49.700 --> 00:33:53.510
back and launch more
jobs again off of that.

00:33:53.510 --> 00:33:57.860
And then use preemptible VMs for
the shorter, independent jobs.

00:33:57.860 --> 00:33:59.600
So they chose
Google Cloud because

00:33:59.600 --> 00:34:02.495
of our Skylake processors,
our fast local SSDs,

00:34:02.495 --> 00:34:04.370
our commitment to
open-source, and of course,

00:34:04.370 --> 00:34:05.161
that crazy pricing.

00:34:07.960 --> 00:34:11.290
So I'd like to offer you
guys the chance to look up

00:34:11.290 --> 00:34:14.080
some of these blog posts and a
few more that I didn't mention

00:34:14.080 --> 00:34:17.275
on things like satellite
image processing and genomics.

00:34:20.500 --> 00:34:26.500
And we also have some great
talks this week at Next '18.

00:34:26.500 --> 00:34:28.270
The top one actually
happened already,

00:34:28.270 --> 00:34:30.070
but all of our sessions
will be recorded

00:34:30.070 --> 00:34:31.840
and available on
YouTube very quickly--

00:34:31.840 --> 00:34:35.409
actually, yesterday's
sessions are available today.

00:34:35.409 --> 00:34:37.030
But we do have two
more coming up.

00:34:37.030 --> 00:34:39.100
So "Research Workloads
on Preemptible VMs"

00:34:39.100 --> 00:34:42.100
is about doing computing
on preemptible VMs,

00:34:42.100 --> 00:34:43.929
really taking advantage of them.

00:34:43.929 --> 00:34:46.000
And that's with one of
our product managers

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:49.510
and a couple of people from
Two Sigma, a financial services

00:34:49.510 --> 00:34:50.552
customer.

00:34:50.552 --> 00:34:52.510
As well as "Computing
with Power Not Previously

00:34:52.510 --> 00:34:54.719
Possible with BigQuery
and Compute Engine."

00:34:54.719 --> 00:34:57.012
This is actually touching
on Slurm specifically.

00:34:57.012 --> 00:34:58.970
So SUNY Downstate Medical
Center and University

00:34:58.970 --> 00:35:02.230
of South Carolina are using
Slurm auto-scaling clusters

00:35:02.230 --> 00:35:05.200
in the Cloud to run their
neuroscience research

00:35:05.200 --> 00:35:07.290
and genomics research,
respectively.

00:35:07.290 --> 00:35:09.690
And they'll be talking
on a panel tomorrow.

00:35:12.300 --> 00:35:13.530
So that's all I had.

00:35:13.530 --> 00:35:15.670
Does anybody have any questions?

00:35:15.670 --> 00:35:18.690
If not, please dig into those
codelabs labs and tutorials

00:35:18.690 --> 00:35:19.290
and have fun.

00:35:19.290 --> 00:35:22.640
